<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelfrith of Northumbria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 616</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Æthelfrith, Æthelfrith of Bernicia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelfrith was king of Bernicia from about 593 and, from about 604, also of Deira, uniting them as the first ruler of Northumbria. He defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> at Degsastan in 603 and the Britons at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chester">Chester</a> around 615, but was killed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raedwald-of-east-anglia">Rædwald of East Anglia</a> at the River Idle in 616. His sons later ruled Northumbria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelfrith-of-northumbria.jpg" alt="Æthelfrith of Northumbria" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Britain_peoples_circa_600.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Æthelfrith was the son of Æthelric and grandson of Ida, and he apparently succeeded Hussa as king of the Bernicians in 592 or 593, with his capital at Bamburgh. His accession may have involved dynastic rivalry and the exile of Hussa's relatives. The genealogies attached to some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/historia-brittonum">Historia Brittonum</a> say that he ruled Bernicia for twelve years and Deira for another twelve, which can be read as Bernicia alone from about 592 to 604. His predecessors are obscure, and he is the earliest Bernician ruler about whom significant details are known. The historian Frank Stenton wrote that the continuous history of Northumbria, and indeed of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, begins with his reign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> praises his successes against the Britons while noting his paganism, and says he conquered more territory from them than any other king, making them tributary or driving them out and planting English in their places. It may have been Æthelfrith who destroyed the British army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-catraeth">Battle of Catraeth</a>, about 600, described in the poem Y Gododdin. In 603 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedan-mac-gabrain">Áedán mac Gabráin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> led a great army against him, and according to Bede Æthelfrith won a crushing victory at Degsastan with an inferior force, though his brother Theodbald was killed.</p><p>About 604 he gained control of Deira; the circumstances are unknown, though the exile of Edwin, son of the former king Ælla, suggests conquest. D. P. Kirby suggested instead a formalisation of an existing relationship between the two kingdoms. His son Oswald was born about 604 to Acha of Deira, Edwin's sister.</p><p>Probably between 613 and 616 he attacked the kingdom of Powys and won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-chester">Battle of Chester</a>, in which the Powysian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selyf-ap-cynan">Selyf Sarffgadau</a> was killed. Before the battle he massacred the monks of Bangor-Is-Coed, who had assembled to pray for the Britons; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> gives about 1200 dead and fifty escaping. Historians have debated its importance, and John T. Koch says the older view that it split the Britons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> from those of the north is now outdated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Edwin sought safety in several kingdoms and finally in East Anglia under King Rædwald. Æthelfrith sent messengers with money and threats to have him killed or handed over. Rædwald at first agreed but was dissuaded by his wife, and instead raised an army. About 616 Æthelfrith was defeated and killed at the Battle of the River Idle; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says his army was the smaller, because Rædwald gave him no time to gather all his forces. Edwin was placed on the throne, and Æthelfrith's sons took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a>. His line was restored to power in the 630s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Æthelfrith” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 603, however, his large army was crushed by <b>Æthelfrith of Northumbria</b> at the Battle of Degsastan, after which, Bede wrote, no king of the Scots in Britain dared to make war on the English.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dal-riata">Dal Riata · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They were first joined around 604 by <b>Æthelfrith of Bernicia</b>, who defeated Áedán mac Gabráin of Dál Riata at the Battle of Degsastan in 603 and the Britons near Chester about 615.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 615 <b>Æthelfrith of Northumbria</b> defeated the Britons at the Battle of Chester, where King Selyf ap Cynan of Powys was killed and, according to Bede, about 1,200 monks of Bangor-on-Dee who had come to pray for the British army were slaughtered.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-powys">Kingdom of Powys · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its leading king, Aedan mac Gabrain, was ordained by Columba of Iona according to Adomnan, and campaigned widely in northern Britain before his defeat by <b>Aethelfrith</b> of Bernicia at Degsastan in 603.</blockquote><a href="/state/cenel-ngabrain">Cenel nGabrain</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Aethelfrith</b> defeated Aedan of Dal Riata at Degsastan in 603 and the Britons at Chester, then seized Deira.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bernicia">Kingdom of Bernicia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in River Idle, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.45,-0.82&amp;year=606" aria-label="River Idle on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Æthelfrith of Northumbria"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4958 5559h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4958 5559h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=53.45,-0.82&amp;year=606">What was here in 606 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
